Original commit body from 5c0006a7ef1aaeb3087f2450f50ddc4c4fa9bef6:

The external_potential bucket was empty on every repository measured,
while the code hid it by default for "tending to dominate". F3 Filter 1
(4e26cf63f0, 2026-05-13) skipped every edge with is_resolved=False,
reading ADR-0028's "the dst could not be resolved" as a receiver guess.
ADR-0037 ruling 1 (2026-06-10) made is_resolved mean in-repo-ness, so
every edge into an external placeholder is is_resolved=False by
definition, and the bucket's own entry test (an external-boundary dst)
admitted only edges Filter 1 then dropped: 1,851 of 1,851 on
full-stack-fastapi-template, 76,015 of 76,015 on the self-survey. The
unit test that pinned Filter 1 built an is_resolved=True edge into an
external dst, a cell ADR-0037 says is never produced.

Filter 1 now skips what it was written to skip: a receiver-unresolved
placeholder whose module slot names nothing (22,503 edges on the
self-survey). A call into a named module the catalogue does not classify
reaches the bucket, its stated purpose. Filter 2 (the closed-world skip)
and the stdlib_other filter are live again for the same reason.

Measured, pinned dev vs branch, io-boundaries on
full-stack-fastapi-template (production files): external_potential
0 -> 376 chains; total_io_edges 29 -> 29 and every classified bucket
identical. The bucket stays disclosed-only and hidden from text output.

The three unit tests that encoded the old filter are rewritten, and the
descriptions that called the bucket "receiver-unresolved noise"
(io_boundary_types, the CLI text, the spec, concept-axes) now say what
it holds.

Addresses INV-toguh.

